###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# Inference on an NVIDIA V100: 13 ms per batch of 50 QM9 molecules
# (inferenceHardware on the QM9 result), 14 ms per ethanol evaluation and
# 15 ms per aspirin MD step in the spectra simulations (held in the encoding
# input; those models have no tabulated error). Training cost is not
# reported.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - PaiNN extends message passing to rotationally equivariant representations; no derivation from a registry method is stated.
# - Only U0 of the twelve QM9 targets is encoded. The QM9 test set is 'the remaining data' of about 130k molecules.
# - The spectra models' errors are in the inset table of Fig. 5 and are not encoded; ethanol and aspirin spectra are not accuracy metrics. The paper gives per-molecule times only; no per-atom time is derived.
# - The ferrocene models (GFN2-xTB data, cutoffs 2.5 to 4.0 A) are not encoded.
# - The DFT levels of QM9 and MD17 are not described in this paper.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: datacite).
# - Evaluation roles and data, base models, inference timings with their system sizes, and the source location of each value are recorded in the encoding input (artifacts/kg/encoding/specs/schutt2021.yaml) until the schema has terms for them.

